            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for overseas, then the Mandarin for to experience for yourself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: wǎngqiú",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǎngqiú — \"tennis\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ xǐhuan wǎngqiú — I like tennis."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǎngqiú is built from 网 'a net' and qiú 'a ball', both inherited from Middle Chinese, mjangX and gjuw (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'a net' and the second 'a fine jade'; under another spelling of the second it glosses a ball of fur that is struck."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for what I like."
            }
          ]
